{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.List.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.List.Perm.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.NormNum"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.objective_blindness_does_not_transfer_to_constrained_optimum","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.objective_blindness_does_not_transfer_to_constrained_optimum","kind":"theorem","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LetterSumBlindness.lean#L190-L220","doc":"**The exclusion does not reach a constrained optimum whose constraints\nsee the word.**\n\nExhibited concretely: an objective that cannot see the word at all (`obj`\ntakes only a point of `X`), two words in the SAME class (`cls` is constant),\nand yet two different optimal values — because the feasible sets differ.\n\nThis is why §1–§3 do not rule out using an embedding energy as the COST\nVECTOR `t` of the operator-selection LP/SDP while the Hecke Gram matrix `G`\nsupplies the constraints. The four gluon candidates have different Markov\ntraces, hence different `G`, hence different feasible sets; minimising even a\ncompletely class-blind objective over four different feasible sets can give\nfour different answers.\n\nRead this as a prune that does NOT fire, not as a positive result. It says\nthe route is not excluded. It does not say the route works, and it does not\nsupply the per-block geometric cost `t_λ` that the route would need. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.bridgeClass_perm_blind","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.bridgeClass_perm_blind","kind":"theorem","line":178,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LetterSumBlindness.lean#L178-L185","doc":"The lattice class used by the bridge is itself letter-sum-type in its\nfirst coordinate (`m` = exponent sum) and constant in its second (`ℓ` = strand\ncount), so it is permutation-blind — which is what puts the bridge in the\nscope of §3 in the first place. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.factorsThrough_blind_forall","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.factorsThrough_blind_forall","kind":"theorem","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LetterSumBlindness.lean#L161-L176","doc":"**No choice of downstream function repairs it — in particular, no\nmodulus.**\n\nIn the bridge, the cusp modulus `τ` enters only when the lattice class is\nturned into a geodesic and the geodesic into an energy, i.e. entirely inside\n`energy`. Universally quantifying over `energy` therefore says: deforming `τ`\n— to the Neumann-Zagier deformed cusp shape, or to anything else, inside that\nfamily or outside it — cannot separate two words that already share a class.\n\nThis is the Lean form of the probe's `no_modulus_can_fix_the_bridge_order_blindness`,\nand it is strictly stronger than the numerical statement: it quantifies over\nall moduli rather than sampling three. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.factorsThrough_blind","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.factorsThrough_blind","kind":"theorem","line":151,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LetterSumBlindness.lean#L151-L159","doc":"**Factoring through a class map is blindness.**\n\n`cls` is the lossy step (for the Bring→O'Hara bridge: word ↦ lattice class\n`(m, ℓ) = (writhe, strands)`); `energy` is anything computed downstream of it.\nTwo words with the same class get the same value. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.crossingEnergy_perm_blind","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.crossingEnergy_perm_blind","kind":"theorem","line":139,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LetterSumBlindness.lean#L139-L146","doc":"Crossing ENERGY is a letter-sum (with `f` the per-crossing weight `+q` or\n`-1/q`), hence permutation-blind by the same invariant. Stated over a generic\ncommutative ring so it is a fact about the SHAPE of the functional, not about\nthe particular values `q` and `-1/q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.crossingCount_perm_blind","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.crossingCount_perm_blind","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LetterSumBlindness.lean#L132-L137","doc":"Crossing COUNT is a letter-sum (with `f ≡ 1`), hence permutation-blind.\nThis is the invariant behind the owner's objection that the selection rule\nshould not be minimum crossing number. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.no_letterSum_separates","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.no_letterSum_separates","kind":"theorem","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LetterSumBlindness.lean#L120-L128","doc":"**No letter-sum functional separates two words that are reorderings of\neach other.** The contrapositive form, which is how the exclusion is used:\nif a selector is defined by summing a weight over the letters, then it takes\nthe same value on `σ₁σ₀` and `σ₀σ₁`, whose closures are measurably\ndifferent links. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.letterSum_perm_invariant","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.LetterSumBlindness.letterSum_perm_invariant","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LetterSumBlindness.lean#L109-L118","doc":"**Any sum over letters is invariant under reordering them.**\n\n`f` is an arbitrary per-letter weight into an arbitrary commutative monoid:\nthe crossing count (`f ≡ 1`), the crossing energy (`f` = `+q` or `-1/q` by\nsign), the writhe (`f` = `±1`), a `q`-dependent weight, anything. The\nconclusion is the same for all of them. ","signatureChunk":0}]}